lon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184065 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184065 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add -\\mathbf{w} to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184065 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184065 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add -\\mathbf{w} to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom {\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom {\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184761 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184761 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ with coefficients in $\\mathbb{R}$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184761 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **$W$ does not contain the zero element.*** \n\nIndeed, the zero polynomial has degree $0$, which is less than $5.$\n\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- For any polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $5$ (or greater), the corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ has the same degree. $\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184761 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **$W$ does not contain the zero element.*** \n\nIndeed, the zero polynomial is not a polynomial of degree $5$ or greater. Its degree is undefined (or is assigned $-\\infty$ by convention).\n\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- For any polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $5$ (or greater), the corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ has the same degree. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184762 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha 0=0$ for any $\\alpha \\in R$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)+(-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184762 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha \\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha (\\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0})+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184762 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A8}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)+(-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184762 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha (\\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A8}}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0})+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184763 1071 "Let $C$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184763 1071 "Let $C$ be the set of functions $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184763 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in C$ by a scalar is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =-1$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=-f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $C$ contains the zero element. Indeed, the function $e(x),$ such that $e(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R,$ belongs to $C.$ This function is the zero element of $C$ since $e(x)+f(x)=f(x)+e(x)=f(x)$ for any $f(x)\\in C.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in C.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is commutative. $\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186184763 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in C$ by a scalar is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =-1$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=-f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- $C$ contains the zero element. Indeed, the function $e(x),$ such that $e(x)=0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ belongs to $C.$ This function is the zero element of $C$ since $e(x)+f(x)=f(x)+e(x)=f(x)$ for any $f(x)\\in C.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in C.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186184764 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{x}$ for some $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\ma