{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186323706 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $w+w=w$, then $w=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add }(-w)\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186323706 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }(-\\mathbf{w})\\tex