atrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \nFind a basis $B$ of  $\\text{Col}(W).$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186345025 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \nFind a basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of  $\\text{Col}(W).$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186345025 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(W),$ we reduce the matrix $W$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{w}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{w}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186345025 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(W),$ we reduce the matrix $W$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186345026 1071 "The matrix $U$ is given below. Let ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{u}}_{2}$, and ${\\mathbf{u}}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $U.$\nDetermine if $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(U).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${\\mathbf{u}}_{2}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186345026 1071 "The matrix $U$ is given below. Let ${u}_{1}$, ${u}_{2}$, and ${u}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $U.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{u}_{1},{u}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(U).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${u}_{2}={x}_{1}{u}_{1}+{x}_{3}{u}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186345026