e distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is commutative. $\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186385831 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$) because **multiplication of $(x,y)\\in Y$ by a scalar is not closed.***   \n\nFor example, $(1,1)\\in Y$ because $1+1=2\\le 3.$ However, if we add the components of $2\\cdot (1,1)=(2,2),$ we get $2+2=4>3.$ So, $2\\cdot (1,1)\\notin Y.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- The zero element in $Y$ is $(0,0).$ ${\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication in $Y$ satisfy the distributive laws. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186386120 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{w}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $4\\mathbf{v}+2\\mathbf{w}.$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186386120 1071 "Let $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $4v+2w.$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186386120 1074 "First, we multiply each component of $\\mathbf{v}$ by $4$ and each component of $\\mathbf{w}$ by $2.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{v}+2\\mathbf{w} & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 18 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20+(-2) \\\\ -12+18 \\\\ 8+(-4) \\\\ -4+6 \\\\ 0+(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 6 \