 whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, and we have one free variables, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}$ into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-11{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186391873 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} \\\\ -11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186391873 1080 "{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} \\\\ -11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186391874 1080 "\\left\\{0\\right\\}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186391874 1080 "{0}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186391875 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-11{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\right\\}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186391875 1080 "{\\begin{bmatrix}-11{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186391876 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186391876 1080 "{\\begin{bmatrix}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186391877 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ -11{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\right\\}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186391877 1080 "{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ -11{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396592 1071 "Let $Y$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree exactly $2.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396592 1071 "Let $Y$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ with coefficients in $\\mathbb{R}$ of degree exactly $2.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396592 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed.*** \n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ Then $p,q\\in Y,$ but \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)={x}^{2}+x+(-{x}^{2})=x\\notin Y\n$$\n\n because $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)=1\\ne 2.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- $1\\cdot p=p$ for all $p\\in Y.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396592 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed.*** \n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ Then $p,q\\in Y,$ but \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)={x}^{2}+x+(-{x}^{2})=x\\notin Y\n$$\n\n because $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)=1\\ne 2.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- $1\\cdot p=p$ for all $p\\in Y.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396599 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396599 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to \\mathbb{R}$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396599 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396599 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396606 1071 "Let $I$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)={2}^{ax},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396606 1071 "Let $I$ be the set of all functions $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to \\mathbb{R}$ of the form $f(x)={2}^{ax},$ where $a\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396606 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$I$ does not contain the zero element.*** \n\nIndeed, $f(x)={2}^{ax}>0$ for all $x\\in R$ for any $a\\in R,$ hence the zero function $z(x)=0\\notin I.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in I.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396606 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$I$ does not contain the zero element.*** \n\nIndeed, $f(x)={2}^{ax}>0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ for any $a\\in \\mathbb{R},$ hence the zero function $z(x)=0\\notin I.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in I.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396722 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials in the real  variable $x$ with coefficients in $Q.$ Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396722 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials in the real  variable $x$ with coefficients in $\\mathbb{Q}.$ Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186396722 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed.*** \n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomial  $f(x)=x.$ Then $f\\in S$, but $\\sqrt{2}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ }x\\notin S$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- The zero function belongs to $S.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in S.$ $\\,✓$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 false] [17592186396722 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed.*** \n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomial  $f(x)=x.$ Then $f\\in S$, but $\\sqrt{2}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ }x\\notin S$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin \\mathbb{Q}.$\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- The zero function belongs to $S.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in S.$ $\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}$" 13194139544976 17592186576411 true] [17592186399587 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$0\\cdot 0=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =(0+1)\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A7}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{addition of integers}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A5}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha 